joining me now is lieutenant colonel  gray green a falklands veteran who is  still serving today uh great to talk to  you what are your memories of that time  and how hard is it on days like today  when you have to relive them  um good morning um  first of all i think it's very important  to  mention that we're here today to  remember the sacrifice of the 255  military personnel uh that were killed  in the falklands war and also to  remember the families  of the fallen  and indeed to remember  those that were wounded both the  physical injuries and the injuries that  are unseen and those that we have to  support  as we go through the next few decades  for myself i was 19 in the falklands war  we'd just completed  a winter deployment in the arctic so we  felt very well trained to operate pretty  much anywhere in the world and we were  on leave and we got the call  to come back to barracks and  then three or four days later we were  sailing south 8 000 miles to the  auckland islands  that must have been quite a thing at  such a tender age were you expecting  seeing active service really at that age  i mean obviously the situation with  northern ireland was ongoing wasn't it  but  um as you say you were yeah  indeed yeah i think um  you you don't expect to go on operations  but you do hope to go on operations and  that's  absolutely the case today with young  young men and women in the armed  services  when we arrived down in the falklands we  deployed  and ashore and to  san carlos  and very soon after that  the unit i was with 4-2 commander and  k-company we were helicoptered forward  to mount kent  the role there was to reinforce 2-2  special air service who were in a  a firefight with argentinian commandos  and that was my first  experience of being  on an active role because we landed the  helicopters  in a fire fight on the foothills about  kent  and that was quite a hairy experience  but i think going forward from there  one of the things that's often forgotten  about  deployments of that kind is is survival  is the harsh conditions and is the  ability of  the british armed forces to go to  environments all over the world and  particularly the falklands and to be  able to survive without kits and  equipment for extended periods of time  and then from that be able to go forward  and  fight the enemy  and i can give you  a couple of examples of that and one  probably in particular that sticks with  me vividly in my own mind  was it was a fighting patrol  on the foothills of mount harriet just  before the main assault  the aim there was for us to go out as a  small team to harass the enemy to expose  uh uh to to expose their their positions  on on the foothills of harriet so that a  wrecking platoon could identify their  their locations  and that was my first experience of  being shot at and and in fact using my  own weapon in anger  and  yeah it's it's brutal it's aggressive  and  it was an experience that i will never  forget the whole mountain erupted in  fire we expended um  our ammunition and then we retreated out  under very accurate artillery fire from  two nine commander regiment which  covered our retreat back to mount kent  and then from there  we moved on to the main assault two or  three days later  and you talked about those who have  injuries that are unseen that the mental  health impacts of of conflicts are  certainly being  uh examined a lot more and people are a  lot more open about them aren't they um  do you have contact with many veterans  who are still really suffering  psychologically for what they went  through then  yeah i i think um  if if you suffer uh  ptsd from operations those operations  can be from the second world war from  the falcons war or indeed those that are  we recognize now in iraq or afghanistan  and these are young  young men and women that have  experienced something traumatic and they  take those experiences with them for the  rest of their lives and they're the  hidden wounds it's really important  for everyone to try and support these  young men and women as they try to  overcome these wounds and certainly from  a royal marine's perspective  we have our royal marines association  and our romney's charity which is there  to support  all of our royal marines in times of  need and especially those at the moment  who have those hidden injuries  you've obviously stayed within the  british armed forces but but when you  look back at those 255 british troops  that died do you think it was worth it  um the falkland islands are british it's  british sovereign territory and  aggressor  um attacked  and occupied the falkland islands  we went down to  recover the falkland islands  and it was a just war in my view
 

